0 < 1 # fleet/tests probes
1 < 1 
2 < 1 A `probe_*.mjs` file is either a live measurement or a design gate — never a
3 < 1 suite test. A live measurement spends real tokens against a real `claude -p`; a
4 < 1 design gate is model-free and spends only minutes, but it is a reading a person
5 < 1 takes before a change rather than a verdict CI owes on every commit. Either way
6 < 1 the file is deliberately NOT named `test_*.mjs`: `tests/test_fleet_suite.py`
7 < 1 globs `test_*.mjs`, CI has no credentials, and the hermetic sweep
8 < 1 `test_sims_are_hermetic.mjs` never reads a `probe_*.mjs`. The naming is the
9 < 1 whole mechanism — CI and the suite never run these.
10 < 1 
11 < 2 The kata probe spends no tokens and holds no bearer, reaching the hub over the
12 < 2 laptop's own ssh seam (the bearer is sourced on the hub), so it runs on the
13 < 2 LAPTOP and nowhere else — a sandbox reaches the hub through the edge and
14 < 2 cannot file a throwaway project:
15 < 2 
16 < 2     node fleet/tests/probe_kata_facts.mjs
17 < 2 
18 < 1 The current probes:
19 < 1 
20 < 2 - `probe_kata_facts.mjs` — whether the hub still behaves the way the fleet's
21 < 2   contract says it does: the 24 kata facts (#978, #979, #993, #1023 and CLAUDE.md's
22 < 2   seams paragraph), re-read one line per fact against a throwaway project, each
23 < 2   line stamped with the version it was read on. Run it on every kata upgrade,
24 < 2   and before any plan that touches `fleet/kata-client.mjs` — those facts are
25 < 2   what that file's shape is argued from, and nothing in the suite talks to a
26 < 2   hub, so an upgrade can falsify any of them with the tree still green. It
27 < 2   removes its `probe-kata-facts-*` project with the very purge ladder its last
28 < 2   fact measures. Exit 0 every fact holds, 1 at least one drift, 2 the hub was
29 < 2   unreachable or the project was left behind.
30 < 1 - `probe_exe_facts.mjs` — whether exe.dev's lobby still behaves the way
31 < 1   `fleet/RUNBOOK.md`'s §Traps (*Tags, keys and names*, *Reading the lobby*)
32 < 1   and `fleet/CONTRACT.md`'s `exe.dev facts (measured)` list say it does: the
33 < 1   twelve lobby facts, re-read one line per fact against the laptop's own ssh
34 < 1   seam (`ssh exe.dev`), each line stamped with the sha256 of `help all
35 < 1   --json` — exe.dev exposes no version verb, no `--version` and no
36 < 1   changelog, so that digest is the only version marker there is — and the
37 < 1   UTC date. It creates three throwaway VMs (`probe-exe-facts-<stamp>`, 1 vCPU and 2 GB —
38 < 1   the lobby's floor — and two copies of it) and removes every one of them as its last mutating verbs, and
39 < 1   it refuses outright, issuing no `new`, when a `fleet-r*` VM is already
40 < 1   listed — a live run's VM, never to be created beside. Run it on a
41 < 1   verb-drift finding from the doctor's `verb-drift` row or the launch line,
42 < 1   and before any plan touching `fleet/launch.mjs` or `fleet/lobby.mjs` — the
43 < 1   facts here are what those files' shape is argued from. Its exit codes:
44 < 1   exit 0 every fact holds, 1 at least one drift or unreadable fact, 2 the
45 < 1   lobby was unreachable, a live run's VM was listed, or a throwaway was left
46 < 1   behind.
