48 < 1 ## Hand-run: the Jev authoring probes (need `bun`)
49 < 1 
50 < 1 The bridge's `sim_env()` PATH holds node, python3, git, bash and sh only, so four
51 < 1 probes of the Bun authoring tools stay outside the suite (operator pick, #1447).
52 < 1 Each runs against a local stand-in Jev on 127.0.0.1 and spends nothing. Run all
53 < 1 four before any change to `skills/ultrawrite/stories/gate_jev.ts`,
54 < 1 `jev_checks.ts`, `jev.ts` or `skills/ultrawrite/stories/questions.json`:
55 < 1 
56 < 1     for c in record agreement; do node fleet/tests/gate_jev_probe.mjs $c; done
57 < 1     for c in pinned-high pinned-low no-base; do node fleet/tests/gate_jev_base_probe.mjs $c; done
58 < 1     for c in own-files own-only rollback typo; do node fleet/tests/gate_jev_reading_probe.mjs $c; done
59 < 1     for c in bundle map decompose understanding; do node fleet/tests/jev_calls_probe.mjs $c; done
60 < 1 
61 < 1 - `gate_jev_probe.mjs` — `record`: one request asking the five clause keys,
62 < 1   the verdict record left alone and one `gate_rounds` entry written;
63 < 1   `agreement`: the census's two lines over two records. Prints `GATE JEV <case> OK`.
64 < 1 - `gate_jev_base_probe.mjs` — prints one JSON line (`verdict`, `pinned`,
65 < 1   `asked_pinned`, `state_base`); `pinned` is asked only when the diet has a
66 < 1   `base`. Exits 1 only when it could not run; read the line.
67 < 1 - `gate_jev_reading_probe.mjs` — prints one JSON line (`requests`, `asked`,
68 < 1   `state_base`, `caught_M1`, `verdict`, `pinned`, `round`) for policy.json's
69 < 1   `gate_reading` (#1497): `own-files` sends only the sibling entry, unmarked, and
70 < 1   asks `caught_v2`; `own-only` sends `files` as `[]` and asks no `pinned`;
71 < 1   `rollback`, on a copy with both cells set back, sends both entries and asks the
72 < 1   old `caught`; `typo`, on a copy whose `caught` cell names a key no question has
73 < 1   (`caught_v9`), asks the old `caught` and records `reading.caught` as `caught`.
74 < 1   The round names its `reading`. Exits 1 only when it could not run.
75 < 1 - `jev_calls_probe.mjs` — the state `jev_checks.ts` sends Jev at each stage.
76 < 1   Prints `JEV CALLS <case> OK`.
77 < 1 
78 < 1 Everything else the Flock's scripted runs used to probe by hand (catch-up,
79 < 1 deletes, provenance, the Jev trials) is `test_flock_runs.mjs`, in the suite.
80 < 1 
81 < 1 ## Jev's peer-rewrite read, live
82 < 1 
83 < 1 `jev_peer_probe.mjs` asks the real Jev (key in `~/.ultrapowers/typesafe.env`; it spends a few calls)
84 < 1 the `flock_peer_rewrite` question four ways, with five replays each:
85 < 1 - run-296's L:loose rewrite of `preview.ts` exactly as the engine sent it, as a bare agent name
86 < 1   (reported, not judged);
87 < 1 - the same rewrite with the loose-ends side's title and reason, which must read `supersedes` or
88 < 1   `keeps` in a majority;
89 < 1 - run-277's run-together words, which must still read `loses`;
90 < 1 - an unrelated same-file rewrite (the loose-ends builder adds a line the reported problem never asked
91 < 1   for), which must read `loses`.
92 < 1 
93 < 1 Run it before any change to that question, to `factory/flock/engine.mjs`'s `peerRewrites`, or to the
94 < 1 `flock.jev_peer_rewrite` policy:
95 < 1 
96 < 1     node fleet/tests/jev_peer_probe.mjs 5
97 < 1 
